@prefix ds: <https://ds.canonical.com/>.
@prefix anatomy: <https://anatomy.canonical.com/>.
@prefix dt: <https://dt.canonical.com/>.

ds:global_dataviews.component.query_chip a ds:Component;
    ds:name "QueryChip";
    ds:summary "A query chip shows one clause as a row of parts that are read together and changed one at a time, such as a field, an operator and a value. Its parts are its children: a **QueryChip.Segment** for each settled part, and at most one **QueryChip.Entry** for the part being typed. Unlike **Chip**, whose value is one piece of text and whose action is filtering or dismissal, each part of a query chip can be chosen and changed on its own. It serves the clauses of a query bar, the conditions of a saved view and the matchers of a rule. It knows nothing about queries: its host composes the parts and hears which one was activated.";
    ds:anatomyDsl "---\n# TODO implementation: no reference in @canonical/react-ds-global or react-ds-global-form\n# The root is an unclipped row: the body, which holds the parts, and beside it the remove\n# control. Neither the root nor the body is a button; a Segment is a button once scripts run,\n# and only when it has onActivate.\n# The `unity` property chooses the body: `separated`, the default, where each Segment is its\n# own target, or `joined`, where the chip is one target. The body's gap separates the parts:\n# 2 px of the surface under `separated`, 1 px of the line colour under `joined`. The\n# implementation reads each component token first and the value bound here as its fallback.\nnode:\n  uri: global_dataviews.component.query_chip\n  styles:\n    layout.type: inline-flex\n    layout.align: center\n    # Between the body and the remove control: 8 px, so the body's ring, which reaches 5 px\n    # past the body, never draws over the remove control.\n    spacing.gap: dimension.100\n    typography.font: typography.text.secondary\n    typography.whiteSpace: nowrap\n  edges:\n    - switch:\n        on: props\n        cases:\n          - node:\n              # unity: separated. Once scripts run, and while a part is interactive, it\n              # carries role=\"group\", named by `label`. It paints nothing, so the surface\n              # beneath shows through the gap between parts.\n              role: separated body\n              styles:\n                layout.type: inline-flex\n                layout.align: center\n                # The ends of the first and last parts are rounded by clipping the body:\n                # no key sets one corner. --query-chip-radius\n                appearance.radius: dimension.radius.full\n                layout.overflow: hidden\n                # --query-chip-background\n                appearance.background: transparent\n                # --query-chip-gap\n                spacing.gap: dimension.025\n                # 24 px, the minimum target size of WCAG 2.5.8. --query-chip-min-height\n                size.min.height: dimension.300\n                # One ring around the clause while focus is within the body; @focus stands\n                # for :focus-within here, which the DSL does not name. The 3 px ring sits\n                # 2 px outside the body, so it is drawn against the surface beneath the chip,\n                # not the selected fill: 4.93:1 (light) and 5.00:1 (dark) on\n                # color.background, and 4.64:1 (light) or 5.52:1 (dark) on\n                # color.foreground.input, where an inset ring on\n                # color.foreground.secondary.active would give 3.33:1 and 2.97:1.\n                appearance.outline.style@focus: solid\n                appearance.outline.color@focus: color.focusRing\n                appearance.outline.width@focus: dimension.stroke.thickness.large\n                appearance.outline.offset@focus: dimension.025\n              edges:\n                - node:\n                    uri: global_dataviews.subcomponent.query_chip-segment\n                  relation:\n                    cardinality: \"1..*\"\n                    slotName: default\n                - node:\n                    uri: global_dataviews.subcomponent.query_chip-entry\n                  relation:\n                    # At most one: the part being typed.\n                    cardinality: \"0..1\"\n                    slotName: default\n          - node:\n              # unity: joined. The body's fill is the line colour, which shows through its\n              # 1 px gap as a line between parts; the chip is one target, so hover, selected\n              # and invalid show on every part at once.\n              role: joined body\n              styles:\n                layout.type: inline-flex\n                layout.align: center\n                appearance.radius: dimension.radius.full\n                layout.overflow: hidden\n                # No symbol declares the reference design's line colour.\n                # --query-chip-line-color\n                appearance.background: \"#c5c5c5\"\n                appearance.background@invalid: color.border.error\n                spacing.gap: dimension.stroke.thickness.medium\n                appearance.border.style: solid\n                appearance.border.width: dimension.stroke.thickness.medium\n                appearance.border.color: \"#c5c5c5\"\n                appearance.border.color@invalid: color.border.error\n                # 26 px, which no symbol declares.\n                size.min.height: 1.625rem\n                # The same outside ring as the separated body.\n                appearance.outline.style@focus: solid\n                appearance.outline.color@focus: color.focusRing\n                appearance.outline.width@focus: dimension.stroke.thickness.large\n                appearance.outline.offset@focus: dimension.025\n              edges:\n                - node:\n                    uri: global_dataviews.subcomponent.query_chip-segment\n                  relation:\n                    cardinality: \"1..*\"\n                    slotName: default\n                - node:\n                    uri: global_dataviews.subcomponent.query_chip-entry\n                  relation:\n                    cardinality: \"0..1\"\n                    slotName: default\n      relation:\n        cardinality: \"1\"\n    - node:\n        # Present when the host passes onRemove or removeHref: a native <button>, or a link\n        # to removeHref, named by removeLabel. It sits beside the body, outside its\n        # clipping, so its focus ring shows whole. Its target is at least 24 by 24 px. On a\n        # read-only chip in a roving context it is the chip's roving item.\n        role: remove control\n        styles:\n          layout.type: inline-flex\n          layout.align: center\n          layout.justify: center\n          size.min.width: dimension.300\n          size.min.height: dimension.300\n          appearance.radius: dimension.radius.full\n          # The plain fill, not the modifier channel: the remove control stays neutral on a\n          # refused chip, on the fill its icon symbol is paired with.\n          appearance.background: color.foreground.secondary\n          appearance.background@hover: color.foreground.secondary.hover\n          appearance.background@active: color.foreground.secondary.active\n          # Disabled with the chip.\n          appearance.background@disabled: color.foreground.secondary.disabled\n          interaction.cursor: pointer\n          appearance.outline.style@focus: solid\n          appearance.outline.color@focus: color.focusRing\n          appearance.outline.width@focus: dimension.stroke.thickness.large\n          appearance.outline.offset@focus: dimension.025\n        edges:\n          - node:\n              uri: global.component.icon\n              props:\n                icon: close\n              styles:\n                size.width: dimension.200\n                size.height: dimension.200\n                typography.color: color.icon.onForegroundSecondary\n            relation:\n              cardinality: \"1\"\n      relation:\n        cardinality: \"0..1\"";
    ds:anatomyClassic "";
    ds:usage "### When to use\n\n- One clause has parts that a person changes independently: `status · is any of · deployed, ready`.\n- The parts need different emphasis, such as an operator in its own tone.\n\n### When not to use\n\n- A single value: use **Chip**.\n- A read-only label: use **Tag**.\n- A free choice among options: use **ComboboxInput** or **SelectInput**.";
    ds:guidelines "### Accessibility\n\n- The chip is a row of two parts: its body, which holds its children, and, beside it, an  \noptional remove control. Neither the chip nor its body is a button, so no interactive content  \nis ever nested inside one.\n- Once scripts run, and while the chip holds a Segment with `onActivate` or an Entry, the body  \ncarries `role=\"group\"`, named by `label`: the whole clause, with each symbol spoken, such as  \n“status is any of deployed, ready”, which the host takes from its messages. Such a chip  \nlives only inside a roving context, such as QueryInput’s toolbar. A chip whose Segments have  \nno `onActivate` and which holds no Entry is read-only: its parts are plain text, and its one  \ntab stop is its remove control. Before scripts run, every part is plain text.\n- The body shows one focus ring around the clause while focus is within it, including when its  \nEntry has focus. The chip is selected while focus is within its body and it holds no Entry;  \nit has no `selected` property, so the selected fill never shows without focus, and the fill  \nis an additional cue to the ring. Parts draw no ring of their own.\n- The ring is 3 px of `color.focusRing`, 2 px outside the body, so it is drawn against the  \nsurface beneath the chip rather than the selected fill. It meets 3:1 in both modes: 4.93:1  \n(light) and 5.00:1 (dark) on `color.background`, and 4.64:1 in light mode and 5.52:1 in dark  \nmode on `color.foreground.input`. An inset ring on `color.foreground.secondary.active` would  \ngive 3.33:1 and 2.97:1, below 3:1 in dark mode. The body sits 8 px from the remove control,  \nand a host keeps neighbouring chips and its entry 8 px apart too, so no ring draws over a  \nneighbour.\n- The chip is at least 24 px tall. The remove control is a native button, or a link when  \n`removeHref` is given, of at least 24 by 24 px, named by `removeLabel`, such as “Remove  \nstatus is deployed”, which the host takes from its messages. It sits outside the body’s  \nclipping, so its own focus ring shows whole. On a refused read-only chip it also takes  \n`aria-describedby` pointing at the first Segment’s `description`, because no part there  \ntakes focus.\n- Inside a roving composite, such as QueryInput’s toolbar, the chip reads the composite’s  \nroving context: the id of the item that holds the tab stop, and a setter. The chip is one  \ntoolbar item: its first Segment, registered as `<id>`, or its Entry while it holds one,  \nregistered as `<id>:entry`, so the chip’s `id` is required there. That item takes  \n`tabindex=\"0\"` while it holds the stop and is focused when the context moves the stop onto  \nit. The chip’s other Segment buttons always take `tabindex=\"-1\"`: their keyboard path is  \nEnter on the chip, which swaps its first Segment, the field, for an Entry, then Tab, and they  \nstay reachable by pointer. The remove control takes `tabindex=\"-1\"` there: it stays reachable  \nby pointer, and Delete or Backspace on the selected chip is its keyboard path. A read-only  \nchip has no interactive parts, so its remove control is its roving item, as `<id>:remove`,  \nand takes `tabindex=\"0\"` while it holds the stop. Outside a roving context the chip is  \nread-only, and its remove control is in the tab order. A disabled chip is not a roving item:  \nthe host skips it on Left and Right and after a removal, and QueryInput falls back to `entry`  \nwhen `activeItem` names a disabled item. Inside a roving context, a chip is interactive or  \nhas `onRemove` or `removeHref`; otherwise it has no roving item, and the host skips it as it  \nskips a disabled chip.\n- An invalid part carries its state in its accessible description: its `description`, such as  \n“Invalid: ”, through `aria-describedby`. While a part is invalid, the chip’s first  \nSegment is also described by that `description`, because focus lands there first while the  \nchip holds no Entry. A refused chip’s reason is the `description` of its first Segment, which  \nshows the glyph and takes focus when focus moves onto the chip.\n- The chip sets the selected fill through a data attribute, never `aria-selected`, which  \nneither a button nor a group supports. Selection reaches assistive technology through focus.\n\n### Without scripts\n\nBefore scripts run, every part is plain text, and an Entry is not rendered. The remove control  \nis a link to `removeHref` only when the host can spell addresses and passes one; otherwise, as  \nunder an in-memory query state, the server renders the control inert, and it works once  \nscripts run.\n\n### States & interaction\n\nThe chip has several interacting modes, so its states are specified here.StateEntered byShown asdefaultSegment fills from `color.foreground.secondary`hoverthe pointer over a Segment buttonthat Segment’s hover step; a plain-text Segment shows noneselectedfocus within the body while it holds no Entryevery Segment takes the active step, with the focus ringinvalida part with `invalid`a Segment takes the error fill and text, and the error glyph, which is Criticality Error’s own; an Entry shows the error underlinerefused`criticality` is `error`every Segment takes the error tone through the modifier channels, the first Segment shows Error’s glyph, and its `description` gives the reasondisabled`disabled`disabled fills and text; it takes no focus\n\n- The initial state is default. An Entry draws its own look, so the chip has no editing state.\n- The table describes the default `unity`, `separated`. When it is `joined`, hover and invalid  \nshow on every part at once, because the chip is one target; selected shows on every part  \nunder both values.\n- Every transition is the host’s. A Segment calls its `onActivate` when its button is  \nactivated, by a click, Enter or Space, and the remove control calls `onRemove`.\n- The host owns the arrow keys, Tab and Delete, and moves the roving tab stop across chips and  \nthe entry through its roving context. Moving onto a chip lands on its roving item: its first  \nSegment, its Entry while it holds one, or a read-only chip’s remove control.\n- When a chip is removed, the host moves focus and the tab stop to the next chip’s roving item,  \nor to the previous chip’s when none follows, or to its entry when no chip remains.\n- Invalid and refused differ. Invalid is a part at fault, bound to that part’s `@invalid`  \nstate, which wins over any tone the channels give. Refused is a clause the source refused as  \na whole: the host sets the chip’s `criticality` to `error` and sets no `condition` on its  \nSegments, so the channels give every Segment the error tone. Hover and selected bind the  \nplain steps, which replace a channel’s tone, so a hovered or selected Segment of a refused  \nchip loses its error fill; the glyph remains the refused cue.\n- Every Criticality value tints the chip through the modifier channels, as the family requires  \nof a consumer; only `error` carries behaviour: it marks the clause refused, puts Error’s glyph  \non the first Segment, and links the reason from that Segment’s `description`.\n\n### Composition\n\nBuilding a clause and editing one are the same mechanism, which the host composes.\n\n- Once a field is chosen, the host renders a draft chip: a Segment for the field, followed by  \nan Entry. The Entry moves right as the operator and then the value are chosen, each settled  \npart becoming a Segment.\n- Editing a part swaps its Segment for an Entry, and committing swaps it back.\n- A chip holds at most one Entry. QueryInput gives that Entry its suggestions through its  \ncontext.\n\n### Unity\n\n`unity` says how the parts hold together, and its values are named for what each is for, not  \nfor how it looks.\n\n- `separated`, the default, is for a clause whose parts people change one at a time, as in a  \nquery being built. Each Segment carries its own fill and is its own hover and error target;  \nselection is the chip’s. The body sets a 2 px gap, `dimension.025`, and paints nothing, so  \nthe surface beneath the chip shows through between parts.\n- `joined` is for a clause people mostly read as a whole, as in the applied criteria of a saved  \nview shown in summary. The chip is one hover and error target: it sets an invalid data  \nattribute while any of its Segments or its Entry has `invalid`, which drives the body’s and  \nevery Segment’s `@invalid`. The body’s fill is the line colour, and it sets a 1 px gap,  \n`dimension.stroke.thickness.medium`, so a line shows between parts; each Segment’s padding  \ngives 6 px on each side of the line.\n\n`unity` changes neither the elements nor the keyboard model: under both, a Segment is a button  \nonly once scripts run and it has `onActivate`.\n\n- A flat form, with square corners and plain text, is not a value: it differs from `separated`  \nonly in how it looks, so it is `separated` with its component tokens remapped by a theme or  \nan application.\n- `unity` is a property, not a modifier family, so it does not cascade. The modifier families a  \nquery chip reads carry the clause’s meaning only.Slot`separatedjoined`Body background`transparent`the line colour; no symbol declares it, #c5c5c5 in the reference designBody gap`dimension.025dimension.stroke.thickness.medium`Body bordernone`dimension.stroke.thickness.medium`, in the line colourBody radius`dimension.radius.full`, clipping the part ends`dimension.radius.full`, clipping the part endsBody minimum height`dimension.300`26 px, which no symbol declaresSegment background`color.foreground.secondary`, with its hover, active, disabled and error stepsthe same; hover, selected and invalid show on every part at onceSegment text`color.text.onForegroundSecondarycolor.text.onForegroundSecondary`Segment inline padding6 px, which no symbol declares6 pxOperator tone, from **Condition**the Segment’s fill and textthe same";
    ds:tier ds:global_dataviews;
    ds:documentationStage ds:tag.needstriage;
    ds:hasSubcomponent ds:global_dataviews.subcomponent.query_chip-segment, ds:global_dataviews.subcomponent.query_chip-entry;
    ds:hasModifierFamily ds:global.modifier_family.criticality;
